  "worked_solution": "The center of the expansion is $x=2,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=0,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $2,$ i.e., $R\\ge 2.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[0,4).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[0,4).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070319/assets/a56e24009403f0177f608ba932c03ab77e8db35e1db3e1221c4fb49efca7219f.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $1\\in [0,4),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $4\\notin [0,4),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $-2\\notin [0,4),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
